  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why is the brake pedal a lever?",
      "a": "The pedal pivots near the top and your foot pushes far from the pivot, so it multiplies your push several times before it reaches the booster and master cylinder.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the brake pedal work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the brake booster do?",
      "a": "The brake booster, or servo, uses engine suction or an electric pump to add force to your push. It makes the brakes feel light.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a brake servo?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the master cylinder?",
      "a": "The master cylinder is a small cylinder full of brake fluid, with a piston pushed by the pedal. It turns your push into fluid pressure that travels to all four wheels.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the brake master cylinder do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do brakes use fluid instead of rods?",
      "a": "Liquid can't be squashed and its pressure spreads equally in all directions, so a thin pipe can carry force round corners to every wheel at once. If air gets in, it squashes and the pedal feels spongy.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is brake fluid used?",
        "What happens if air gets in the brakes?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do the caliper pistons multiply force?",
      "a": "The caliper pistons have a much bigger area than the master piston. The same pressure on a bigger area gives a bigger force, so a gentle push becomes a strong squeeze on the pads.",
      "asks": [
        "How do hydraulic brakes multiply force?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Pressure in a trapped liquid is passed on equally throughout it, an idea called Pascal's principle.",
    "Cars have two separate brake circuits, so if one leaks the other can still stop the car.",
    "Brake fluid slowly absorbs water, which lowers its boiling point, so it is replaced every few years."
  ],
